package Game;

/** The text of the in-game Help, part 1: getting started, the main screen, the Move tab, the Auto tab and the World tab.
 *  (Part 2 is HelpPagesMore: the Me tab, the game systems, the terminal and the reference.) Markup: see HelpContent. Every claim here was
 *  checked against the code; when a button is renamed, search for its name in these two files. Screenshots live in assets/help/ and are
 *  made by tools/HelpShots.java. */
final class HelpPages {
    private HelpPages() { }

    static final String G1 = "Getting started", G2 = "The main screen", G3 = "Move tab", G4 = "Me tab", G5 = "Auto tab", G6 = "World tab", G7 = "Game systems", G8 = "Terminal commands", G9 = "Reference";

    static void register() {
        gettingStarted();
        mainScreen();
        moveTab();
        HelpPagesMore.meTab();
        autoTab();
        worldTab();
        HelpPagesMore.systems();
        HelpPagesMore.terminal();
        HelpPagesMore.reference();
    }

    // ================================================================== getting started
    private static void gettingStarted() {
        HelpContent.add("start", "Welcome", G1, "What this game is and how to use this Help.", "welcome home intro help guide manual contents",
                "lead Welcome, cultivator. This is a living world: about 100,000 other cultivators, beasts, cities and sects go on with their lives week by week, whether or not you are looking. You are one of them. Nobody scripted a plot for you: you decide who to become.",
                "p This Help explains **every button and every feature**. Read it from top to bottom, or jump straight to what you are looking at right now.",
                "cards",
                "card first-steps|New here? Start here|The start-up dialogs and the first minutes of play.",
                "card turn|How a turn works|Weeks, years, seasons, and what costs time.",
                "card screen|The main window|A map of the screen: what each part is for.",
                "card cultivation|Cultivation and realms|Levels, potential, breakthroughs, lifespan.",
                "card move|Move tab|Walk, wait, and the command box.",
                "card me|Me tab|Your character, backpack, family, quests and places.",
                "card auto|Auto tab|Let the game play for you: modes, quests, scripts.",
                "card world|World tab|Browse the world, manage shops and sects, save, settings.",
                "card combat|Fighting|Win chances, fleeing, kill or spare.",
                "card terminal|Terminal commands|Everything can be typed too.",
                "h2 Using this Help",
                "* **Contents** on the left, grouped like the game. Click a title to open it. The search box above finds words anywhere (every word you type must appear on the page).",
                "* **Links** (in green) jump to other pages. Links that start with a picture of a window, such as [[win:stats|Open the Stats window]], open the real thing so you can try it.",
                "* ((◀)) and ((▶)) at the top go back and forward like a browser; ((⌂ Home)) returns here. The buttons at the bottom of a page walk through the Help in reading order.",
                "* Every game window has a small round ((?)) button that opens the page about it, and there is a ((?)) in the status bar at the top of the main window. {F1} opens this page from the keyboard.",
                "* ((☾ Ink)) / ((☀ Paper)) switches between a warm paper page and a dark ink page. Text size follows the Text size setting in [[settings|Settings]].",
                "tip You can leave the Help open next to the game and read while you play: it never pauses or changes the world.",
                "h2 The two-minute version",
                "# Press a direction button or {W} {A} {S} {D} to walk. One step is one **turn**, and one turn is one **week** of game time.",
                "# Click any tile on the map to read about it in the Tile Info panel; ((▶ Move Here)) walks there.",
                "# Your cultivation grows by itself every week (the green bar at the top). When it fills you gain a level; every ten levels is a new **realm**, which needs enough [[cultivation#potential|potential]].",
                "# Quests appear on their own. Ones you must do yourself are **alerts** (the ⚑ badge); the rest your character handles. See [[quests]].",
                "# When you want to go and do something else, hand the controls over: [[auto|Auto]] plays like an NPC; [[scripts|scripts]] do exactly the chores you list.",
                "# ((💾 Save)) any time. Press {F1} for this Help, and ((Take the tour)) below for a lit-up walk around the window.",
                "p [[win:tour|Start the guided tour]] &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; [[tour|About the tour]]",
                "img screen-main.png|The main window: map, tile info, the four tabs of buttons and the event log.",
                "see first-steps screen terminal faq");

        HelpContent.add("first-steps", "Your first life", G1, "The start-up dialogs and the first minutes of play.", "new game start dialog protagonist standard story difficulty brutal hard single multiplayer watch spectate begin",
                "lead The first time you launch the game it asks a few questions. Here is what each one means, and what to do in your first minutes.",
                "h2 Starting the program",
                "p The game starts in a terminal window and asks **Enable map viewer? Y/n**. Answer {y} to open the windowed game (this Help is part of it). Answer {n} to play with typed commands only (see [[terminal]]).",
                "p If you have played before, a **Saved games** list appears: choose one and press ((Continue selected)), or ((New game)). Saves are explained in [[saves]].",
                "h2 How do you want to play?",
                "table Choice|What happens",
                "row Single player|You create one character and play it.",
                "row Multiplayer (2-4 players)|Several human characters are created one after another (each goes through the same character choice).",
                "row Watch the world|No character at all: you spectate. Open any cultivator (click a name) and press ((Control)) to become them.",
                "h2 A new life",
                "p The **A new life** dialog chooses how your character begins:",
                "table Kind|Meaning",
                "row Protagonist|The perfect cultivator: potential 100, an Alchemist. The early realms fly by; the top realm is still thousands of years away.",
                "row Standard (fate rolls you)|A body drawn from the same lottery as everyone else, so most are mediocre. A very poor roll (potential under 20) is rerolled once. Your talent is only a word (dull, ordinary, talented, gifted...) until your first breakthrough.",
                "row Standard (custom)|A sandbox: you choose potential, trait, alignment, gender and name. Nothing is hidden.",
                "row Story|One of eighteen characters with a past and a chain of objectives (see the [[story|Story]] window). Pick the character from the list, then a difficulty.",
                "p **Difficulty** (Story starts only): **Story** is the character as written. **Hard**: potential at 60%, no starting gear, half the money, stranger evil people pick on you more and lost fights are deadlier. **Brutal**: potential rolled between 1 and 20, no gear, no money, a hostile world, hidden potential, and **when you die that is the end** (no rebirth, no taking over another body).",
                "p After that the game offers ((Yes)) / ((No)) to **modify the character**. Yes opens the editor: name, trait, potential, alignment, gender. It is the same editor as the Edit Self button on the Me tab.",
                "img win-start.png|The “A new life” dialog.",
                "h2 Your first minutes",
                "# Read the status bar at the top: your name, realm, a cultivation bar, age and wealth. See [[status-bar]].",
                "# Click around the map. The Tile Info panel on the right tells you what is on each tile. See [[map]] and [[tile-info]].",
                "# Open ((Stats)) on the **Me** tab to see what you are made of: potential, fighting level, gear. See [[stats]].",
                "# Walk (or press ((▶ Move to Selected Tile))) to a tile with herbs or metal, then use the command box: `forage` or `mine`. Working is how a young cultivator earns spirit stones. See [[economy]].",
                "# Look at the quest list ((Quest Log)) to see what your character would do on its own. See [[quests]].",
                "# When you are tired of clicking, try ((Auto)) on the Auto tab, or ((Watch ▸▸)) to let weeks pass until something happens. See [[auto]].",
                "warn In **Brutal** a death is final, and in every mode a bad fight can kill you. Before you attack someone, use the fight preview: type `preview #<id>` or press ((Preview fight)) in [[browse|Browse]]. See [[combat]].",
                "tip For the first year, nobody may start a fight in Originis (the central city): a 52-week truce keeps the opening quiet.",
                "see turn screen cultivation tour");

        HelpContent.add("turn", "How a turn works", G1, "Weeks, years and seasons; what costs time and what is free.", "week year season tick time wait pause speed manual free action turn calendar",
                "lead One turn is one **week**. Each turn the whole world moves once: everyone ages a little, cultivates, travels, fights, trades, marries and dies. In between, the world waits for you.",
                "h2 What happens each week",
                "# Everyone cultivates a little (you too, always).",
                "# Humans act. In **MANUAL** mode the game stops and waits for you to choose one action. In **AUTO** and **HYBRID** mode your character acts by itself (see [[auto]]).",
                "# Everyone else acts: NPCs walk, work, quarrel, hunt, trade and follow their own quests.",
                "# The land ages (beasts and herbs regrow), cities collect tax once a year, wars and tournaments tick on.",
                "h2 What costs a turn and what is free",
                "table Costs the turn|Free (no time passes)",
                "row Moving, waiting, working (`forage`, `mine`, `craft`, `hunt`, `meditate`), buying or selling, talking, sparring, attacking, founding or joining something, taking a quest with ((Do top quest)).|Looking at things: Stats, Backpack, Relations, Quests, Locations, Here, People, lists, Browse, the Help, changing settings, setting duties and policies, scripts editing.",
                "p The status line at the top shows what the game is doing: **◆ Waiting for input** means it is your move; **▶ Simulation running** means weeks are passing by themselves (AUTO, a script, a long walk, Watch).",
                "h2 Years and seasons",
                "* 52 weeks make a year. Your age goes up by one each year, and lifespan is limited: see [[cultivation]].",
                "* A year has four seasons of 13 weeks: spring, summer, autumn, winter. Each season city festivals happen ([[world-events]]); the season also changes how the pictures look.",
                "* Once a year: the **auction** in the first week, taxes, new cities and sects are founded, sects post missions, and the game may autosave. The `time` command (the ((Time)) button on the Auto tab) tells you the date and how many weeks remain to the next auction.",
                "h2 Speed and pause",
                "p The speed buttons in the top-right of the window set how long the game rests between weeks when it is running by itself: 0ms (as fast as possible), 0.1s, 0.5s, 2s, 10s. ((⏸ Pause)) stops everything after the current week; press it again to resume. Pressing a speed button also resumes. See [[speed]].",
                "tip The fastest way to let time pass is `wait 52` (a year in one command) or ((Watch ▸▸)): it runs until a notice, a new alert or an attack stops it. Fights, breakthroughs and new alerts always interrupt automatic play so nothing important slips by.",
                "see speed auto cultivation");

        HelpContent.add("cultivation", "Cultivation and realms", G1, "Levels, minor and major realms, potential, breakthroughs, lifespan.", "level realm major minor potential breakthrough tribulation lifespan age inhibitor qi foundation core nascent immortal cultivate progress bar gate",
                "lead Cultivation is the heart of the game: you grow in power every week. How far you can go depends mostly on your **potential**.",
                "h2 Levels, minor and major realms",
                "p Your **level** runs from 1 to 100. Every ten levels is a **major realm** (Major 0 to Major 10); inside a realm the ten steps are **minor levels**. The status bar shows both: “Major 2.7 Lv27” means Major realm 2, minor level 7, level 27. The Stats window calls minor 1-3 ~~low~~, 4-6 ~~mid~~, 7-9 ~~high~~ and 10 ~~peak~~.",
                "p You gain cultivation every week. When the progress bar (“cult 63%”) fills, you gain a level. The speed depends on your potential (higher is faster), your level (higher levels need much more), the food and wine you have used, and your trait (a Spirit Vein cultivates 1.5 times faster).",
                "table Major|Realm|Potential needed to pass the gate|Lifespan gained",
                "row 0|Qi Cultivation|-|a base of 100 years",
                "row 1|Foundation|20|+100",
                "row 2|Core Formation|37|+200",
                "row 3|Soul Wandering|52|+400",
                "row 4|Nascent Soul|65|+1,900",
                "row 5|Ensoulment|76|+3,700",
                "row 6|Nihility|85|+6,100",
                "row 7|Ascension|92|+9,100",
                "row 8|Half-Immortal|97|+13,700",
                "row 9|Pseudo Immortal|99|+16,900",
                "row 10|True Immortal|100, and a 1 in 5 chance per try|+2.14 billion",
                "h2 Potential",
                "p **Potential** (1-100) is the ceiling of your talent. To pass from one major realm to the next you need the potential in the table. Below it, the breakthrough almost always **fails** (about 14 times out of 15 in the early realms, 9 of 10 later): you fall back one level and your cultivation slows permanently, because every failed attempt adds to your **inhibitor** (each one halves your speed). So a low-potential character stalls, and wasting attempts makes it worse.",
                "p In Standard, Hard and Brutal starts your potential is a **word** (dull, ordinary, talented, gifted, prodigious, heaven-chosen) until you reach Major 1. Potential can rise: a rare chance at each successful breakthrough, from surviving the Forbidden Technique, in haunted grounds and celestial trials, from Potential Pills, and by reincarnation (see [[reincarnation]]).",
                "p **The higher your potential, the less each gain is worth.** A Potential Pill (or any insight) counts as one pill: below the first gate (20) it is worth 4 points, and the cost of every gate is higher than the last. From the last gate to the next it takes **5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15, 18, 22, 26 and 30 pills** (156 in all). What is not a whole point yet is kept as progress ('your potential stirs') and adds up; your own sheet shows it as **41 (+60%)**.",
                "h2 Breakthrough tribulations",
                "p Crossing into major realm 3, 5, 7 and 9 triggers a **tribulation**: you pass with a chance of potential minus 5 for every step beyond realm 3 (never under 10% nor over 90%). Passing brings a bonus to your cultivation; failing throws you back and slows you.",
                "h2 Lifespan",
                "p You have a **maxAge** shown next to your age in the status bar (“age 21/118”). Each realm you break through adds many years. When age reaches the limit you die of old age. The Forbidden Technique burns years of lifespan (see [[combat]]).",
                "h2 Ways to grow faster",
                "* Join a **sect**: members get extra cultivation each week ([[sect]]).",
                "* **Dual cultivation**: a spouse or a best friend in your tile sometimes gives both of you extra cultivation.",
                "* Eat food, drink wine, listen to music: see [[life-arts]]. Meditate on purpose: `meditate` or the Meditate quest.",
                "* Visit dungeons and trials that match your level ([[world-events]]).",
                "* Learn better techniques from a city library ([[market]]), which also makes you stronger in fights ([[techniques]]).",
                "see stats combat reincarnation sect");

        HelpContent.add("tour", "The guided tour", G1, "A short, lit-up walk around the main window.", "tour tutorial welcome first run walkthrough highlight",
                "lead The tour is the quickest way to learn where everything is: a small window explains the main window one part at a time while the part it talks about is lit up.",
                "p It opens by itself the first time you play. Open it again any time:",
                "p [[win:tour|Start the guided tour]]",
                "p It has seven short pages: welcome, the map, Tile Info, the four tabs, the status bar, time and the event log, and where to go next. Pressing ((Next ▶)) moves on, ((Open the Help)) shows the Help page for that step, and the check box at the bottom turns the one-time hints on or off (see [[settings]]).",
                "tip The tour window moves out of the way of what it is lighting up. You can keep playing while it is open.",
                "see start screen settings");
    }

    // ================================================================== the main screen
    private static void mainScreen() {
        HelpContent.add("screen", "The main window", G2, "A map of the screen: what each part is for.", "window layout panels dock status bar tile info tracker log header speed overview",
                "lead The window has six parts. Click the name of a part to read about it.",
                "img screen-main.png|The main window. 1 header, 2 status bar, 3 map and layers, 4 Tile Info and tracker, 5 the tabs of buttons, 6 the event log.",
                "table Part|What it is",
                "row **1. Header** (top)|The title, and on the right the speed buttons and ((⏸ Pause)). See [[speed]].",
                "row **2. Status bar**|Your name, realm, cultivation bar, age, wealth, mode, alerts, notifications, music, place, quest, ((▶ Go)), save state, ((?)) and ((⏻ Quit)). See [[status-bar]].",
                "row **3. Map**|The world, 31 x 31 tiles, with a layer bar above it. Click a tile to read about it. See [[map]].",
                "row **4. Tile Info and NPC Tracker** (right)|Everything about the selected tile, and a short list of cultivators you follow. See [[tile-info]].",
                "row **5. The four tabs** (right, bottom)|[[move|Move]], [[me|Me]], [[auto|Auto]] and [[world|World]]: every button of the game.",
                "row **6. Event log** (bottom)|What is happening in the world and to you, with filters. See [[speed#the-event-log|the event log]].",
                "p You can drag the grey dividers between the parts to make any of them bigger. The window remembers nothing about the layout, but the text size does persist: **Settings, Text size**.",
                "tip Windows opened from the buttons are separate and **never modal**: you can leave several open, move them around and keep clicking in the main window. Opening the same one again simply brings it to the front.",
                "see map tile-info status-bar speed move");

        HelpContent.add("map", "The world map", G2, "Colours, layers, clicking and the legend.", "map layers terrain beasts population level crowd fog quests path legend click double click tile pictures thumbnails hotspot colorblind north compass torus wrap",
                "lead The map is the whole world: 31 x 31 tiles, **north is up**. Each tile is a place you can walk to. The world **wraps around**: walk off the north edge and you arrive on the south edge.",
                "img map-terrain.png|The map with the Terrain colours. The yellow dot is you, the white square is the selected tile.",
                "h2 Clicking",
                "* **Click** a tile to select it (a white outline). The Tile Info panel then describes it, and ((▶ Move Here)) walks there.",
                "* **Double-click** a tile to open a large picture of the place.",
                "* **Hover** over a tile for a small card: name, coordinates, level, how many cultivators and beasts are there.",
                "* Click a cultivator's name in the event log and the character window opens: see [[character]].",
                "p Coordinates are shown as hundreds: tile (15, 15), the centre of the world and the city Originis, is **[1500, 1500]**. Commands like `goto 1500 1500` accept either form.",
                "h2 The layer bar",
                "p Above the map is a bar of switches. The drop-down **Map:** chooses what the tile **colours** mean:",
                "table Colour mode|Shows",
                "row Terrain|Biome and height (sea, beach, desert, tundra, grassland, forest, mountains...). Cities are gold, sects violet, dungeons their own colours (see the Legend). Wilder, higher-level tiles are darker.",
                "row Beasts|How many beasts live there, on a log scale: dark green (few) to red (very many).",
                "row Population|How many cultivators stand there: dark to bright blue.",
                "row Level|The tile's danger level: green (weak) to red (strong).",
                "p The toggle buttons add **overlays** on top (each is on or off independently):",
                "table Toggle|What it does",
                "row Pictures|Shows the picture of a place in Tile Info (needs the ~~Place pictures~~ setting).",
                "row Tile pics|Draws small pictures inside map tiles. They are kept for the whole map, so switching them on costs almost nothing; zoom in for bigger, sharper ones.",
                "row Zoom|1x shows the whole world; each step shows fewer, bigger tiles. The mouse wheel zooms too (on the tile under the pointer). Drag the map to move around. ((Follow me)) keeps your character in the middle while zoomed; dragging the map turns it off.",
                "row Crowd|Outlines crowded tiles: **red** = a hotspot (beasts are ambushing there), **amber** = above 80% of the capacity. See [[world-events#crowds-and-hotspots|hotspots]].",
                "row Fog|Dims every tile you have never visited. Handy for exploring.",
                "row Quests|Little diamond pins on the places your quests point to: **orange** an alert, **cyan** a duty, **grey** a routine quest.",
                "row Path|A dashed line from you to the selected tile with the distance in tiles.",
                "img map-layers.png|Quest pins, a path line, fog of war and crowd outlines switched on.",
                "p ((Legend ▾)) opens the colour key: City, Sect, Lost Ruins, Beast Lair, Martial Trial, Haunted Grounds, Celestial Trial, Spiritual Vein, your position and the wildlands.",
                "h2 What the marks mean",
                "* A **yellow dot** is you. A **cyan dot** is a cultivator you track. The small **N ↑** reminds you that north is up.",
                "* **Gold** tiles are cities, **violet** tiles are sects, and the dungeon colours show ruins, lairs, trials and haunted grounds. A bright tile has more people in it.",
                "tip The setting ~~Colour-blind-safe map colours~~ in [[settings]] swaps the palette for one that keeps places apart without relying on red and green. {O} toggles place pictures and tile pictures at once.",
                "img map-beasts.png|Beasts mode.",
                "img map-population.png|Population mode.",
                "see tile-info painted-map world-overview world-events");

        HelpContent.add("tile-info", "Tile Info and the NPC Tracker", G2, "What the right-hand panel tells you about the selected tile.", "tile info tracker follow track npc cultivators beasts bounties city sect dungeon spirit vein move here weather season picture",
                "lead The panel at the top right describes the tile you selected on the map (or the one you stand on at the start).",
                "img tile-info.png|Tile Info for a city tile.",
                "h2 What it shows, top to bottom",
                "* The **season** and the **weather** there (clear, rain or fog), and where you are.",
                "* A **picture** of the place (click it for a larger view), the **name**, and for a sect its **seal**.",
                "* The **region** it lies in (named seas, ranges, rivers) and the **level** of the tile (★ Hotspot, ✦ Spirit Vein where they apply). Coordinates and biome.",
                "* For a **city**: its lord and its population. For a **sect**: its master and any territory bonus. **Dungeons** say what kind they are (and, for a celestial trial, how many ticks remain). A heaven-sent treasure and the number of **arrays** protecting the place are listed too.",
                "* **Cultivators**: how many are in the tile (and the cap before it becomes overcrowded), the number of **beasts**, and the **bounties** posted.",
                "* The **top five cultivators** by level. Each is a button: click to open their character window ([[character]]).",
                "h2 The buttons",
                "table Button|What it does",
                "row ((▶ Move Here [x,y]))|Walks you there, one tile per turn, stopping on arrival, on a new alert or when attacked. Same as `goto x y`.",
                "row ((City / sect details…))|(only for cities and sects) A window with the full report of the place: lord or master, shops, libraries, arrays, who is there, its history.",
                "h2 The NPC Tracker",
                "p Below Tile Info is a short list of cultivators you chose to **follow**. Each row shows name, level, age and tile; click the name to open them, and ((×)) stops following. A followed cultivator is also a cyan dot on the map. A follower who has died stays in the list as a dagger with their id: ((×)) beside it, or ((Clear deceased)), removes them. Add someone with the ((Track)) button in their character window, the ((Follow)) button in [[browse|Browse]], or `follow #<id>`.",
                "see map character browse");

        HelpContent.add("character", "The character window", G2, "What you see when you click a cultivator's name.", "character window cultivator dialog click name biography track control portrait inspect another npc",
                "lead Click a cultivator's name anywhere (Tile Info, the event log, a table, the tracker) and this window opens. It shows what is public about that person.",
                "img win-character.png|A character window.",
                "p It lists a portrait (if pictures are on), name and title, realm and level, cultivation progress, age, potential (the real number for you; a plain number for others), trait, alignment and gender, profession level, wealth, weapon and armour, kills, items crafted, sect and rank, spouse, location, and the id (used in commands as `#id`).",
                "table Button|What it does",
                "row ((Biography))|Opens the [[biography]] window: charts of that person's life and the events they were part of.",
                "row ((Track))|Adds them to the NPC Tracker and the map ((✓ Tracked)) when already added.",
                "row ((Control))|Become this cultivator. Your current character carries on as an NPC. It asks to confirm (see [[settings]]) and your duty toggles and policies carry over. When you are spectating, Control simply picks someone to play.",
                "row ((Close))|Closes the window.",
                "tip Everything you can do **to** another person (talk, spar, attack, invite, seek) is in [[browse|Browse]], or typed: `talk #123`. See [[terminal-ref|the terminal reference]].",
                "see tile-info browse biography");

        HelpContent.add("status-bar", "The status bar", G2, "Your vital signs at the top of the window.", "status bar name realm cultivation age wealth mode alerts bell notifications music place quest go save quit help potential",
                "lead The strip under the title is your dashboard. It updates every turn.",
                "img status-bar.png|The status bar.",
                "table Item|Meaning",
                "row Your **name**|Your character (a spectator sees “no player”).",
                "row **Major 2.7 Lv27 Trait**|Major realm . minor level, total level, then your trait (what your character is gifted at). See [[cultivation]].",
                "row The **green bar**|Cultivation progress toward the next level, with “cult 63% pot 52” (potential, or a word while it is hidden).",
                "row **age 21/118**|Your age and your lifespan limit.",
                "row **1,250 ss**|Your wealth in spirit stones (spirit crystals and higher coins are converted into stones: 1 crystal = a million stones).",
                "row **MANUAL · duties …**|Your control mode and which duties are on. See [[auto]] and [[duties]].",
                "row ((⚑ 3))|Quest alerts waiting for you. Click to open the [[quests|quest panel]]. Orange means there are some.",
                "row ((🔔 2))|Notifications you have not read. Click for the history ([[notifications]]).",
                "row ((♪ on))|Generated music on or off. Click toggles it; hover to see what is playing. See [[music]].",
                "row **Place [x, y]**|Where you stand.",
                "row **▶ quest name**|Your current top quest.",
                "row ((▶ Go))|Walks to the place of your top quest, or chases the person it names (same as `goquest`).",
                "row **💾 …**|Save state: the last save's name, the game year and how long ago. It turns amber while saving and red if a save **failed** (hover for the reason).",
                "row ((?))|Opens this Help (also {F1}).",
                "row ((⏻ Quit))|Asks: Save and quit, Quit without saving, or Keep playing.",
                "see screen quests notifications saves");

        HelpContent.add("speed", "Speed, pause and the event log", G2, "The speed buttons, Pause, the status text and the event log.", "speed pause 0ms fast slow event log history filter fights economy world search click hover",
                "lead The top-right of the window controls how fast time runs when the game is playing itself; the bottom shows what is happening.",
                "img speed-bar.png|The speed buttons and Pause.",
                "table Control|What it does",
                "row ((⏸ Pause))|Stops the game after the current week (the button turns gold). Press again to resume. Also {P}.",
                "row ((0ms)) ((0.1s)) ((0.5s)) ((2s)) ((10s))|How long the game rests between two weeks when it is running by itself (AUTO, scripts, Watch, long walks, or a spectated world). The chosen speed is highlighted. In MANUAL mode the world still waits for you each turn.",
                "row Status text|**⏸ PAUSED**, **◆ Waiting for input — press a button** (your move), or **▶ Simulation running...**",
                "h2 The event log",
                "p The bottom panel has two tabs. **Events** is the live log of the world. **World History** keeps the big events of the whole run.",
                "img event-log.png|The event log with its filters.",
                "* The check boxes **Fights, Economy, World, Me, Other** hide or show a kind of line. “Me” holds lines that mention you.",
                "* The **search** box shows only lines that contain your text; press Enter.",
                "* **Click a line** to open the first cultivator named in it. **Hover** a line for a short card about that person.",
                "tip If the log scrolls too fast to read, pause, or use [[chronicle|Chronicle]] (searchable history of the world) and [[digest|Digest]] (what happened lately) instead.",
                "see turn chronicle digest");
    }

    // ================================================================== Move tab
    private static void moveTab() {
        HelpContent.add("move", "Move tab", G3, "Walk, work, do quests, talk and fight: every button of the first tab, plus the command box and Help.", "move walk direction pad north south east west diagonal wait goto route command box key wasd arrows numpad travel goquest chase meditate hunt forage mine craft rest sell buy gear pills eat drink equip talk spar do top quest next alert watch help take control",
                "lead The first tab of the dock is where you **act on your turn**. Every button is a typed command in disguise (the commands are listed in the tables), so you can click or type, whichever is quicker. Buttons that cannot work where you stand are greyed out; hover over one to read why.",
                "img dock-move.png|The Move tab.",
                "p When the character is not in MANUAL mode a banner at the top says so and offers ((◀ Take control)) (`stop`): one click and the game waits for you again.",
                "h2 Walk",
                "table Button|Does|Key",
                "row ((↑)) ((↓)) ((←)) ((→))|Move one tile north, south, west, east. North is **up** on the map.|{W} {S} {A} {D} or the arrow keys",
                "row ((NW)) ((NE)) ((SW)) ((SE))|Move diagonally: **one turn**, like the pathfinder.|numpad {7} {9} {1} {3}, or {Home} {PgUp} {End} {PgDn}",
                "row ((Wait))|Stay where you are for one turn. The world moves a week.|{Space} or numpad {5}",
                "row ((▶ Move to Selected Tile))|Walk to the tile you clicked on the map, one tile per turn, until you arrive, a new alert appears or someone attacks you. Same as ((▶ Move Here)) in [[tile-info]] and `goto x y`.|",
                "row ((▶ Go to top quest))|Walk to the place of your first quest, or chase the person it names. Same as the **Go** button in the status bar: `goquest` / `chase #id`.|",
                "row ((Route…))|Asks “x y” and previews that walk: distance, arrival date, dangers on the road. Costs no time: `route x y`.|",
                "row ((Watch ▸▸))|Let weeks pass until something happens to you (a notice, a new alert, an attack), at most a year: `watch`.|{.}",
                "p Each step uses a turn (a week). The world wraps: walking north from the top row brings you to the bottom row.",
                "h2 Do here (uses the turn)",
                "table Button|Does|Command",
                "row ((Meditate))|Cultivate for a week: one and a half cultivation ticks, and each of your four equipped techniques gains a level (up to its potential).|`meditate`",
                "row ((Hunt))|Fight the beasts of this tile for cultivation, materials and bounties. Greyed out when there are none.|`hunt`",
                "row ((Explore))|Explore the Lost Ruins, Beast Lair, Martial Trial, Haunted Grounds or Celestial Trial of this tile: ruins pay treasure and sometimes a technique, a lair is three fights, a trial sharpens your techniques, haunted grounds and celestial trials give cultivation and a chance of potential. Greyed out when the tile has none.|`explore`",
                "row ((Forage)) ((Mine))|Gather herbs / dig metal. Greyed out when the tile has none.|`forage` `mine`",
                "row ((Profession ▾))|A turn of your own trade, or a menu of the seven kinds you can make as an amateur (weapon, clothing, pill, array, food, wine, poison). The trade gathers or buys what it is short of and says why when it cannot: an Alchemist brews pills, a Blacksmith forges, a Tailor sews, a Scribe draws arrays, an Innovative writes a technique once Inspiration is 100 (fights and hunts give it) or enchants a weapon, a Merchant opens or restocks a shop, a Leader founds or claims a city or sect, Chef, Drunken and Poisoner make food, wine and poison, a Musician plays, a Tamer tames, Gatherer and Miner gather, a Friendly talks, a Lucky picks up what the fallen left.|`profession`, `craft [kind]`",
                "row ((Rest ×4))|Wait four turns in a row (an alert or an attack cuts it short).|`wait 4`",
                "row ((Sell)) ((Buy gear))|In a city: sell what you do not use / buy the best gear you can afford.|`sell` `buygear`",
                "row ((Use pills))|Swallow the pills you carry.|`consume`",
                "row ((Eat)) ((Drink))|Food or wine from your goods: a short boost. Greyed out when you carry none (see [[life-arts]]).|`eat` `drink`",
                "row ((Equip best))|Wear the strongest weapon and armour in your backpack.|`equip`",
                "h2 Quests",
                "table Button|Does|Command",
                "row ((Do top quest))|Do the most pressing quest that can be done right where you stand. If none can, it tells you where to go.|`qdo`",
                "row ((Go to next alert))|Walk to the first quest that waits for you (the flag in the status bar).|`goquest #uid`",
                "row ((Quest Log))|The one window for every quest: see [[quests]].|{Q}",
                "h2 People",
                "table Button|Does|Command",
                "row ((Talk)) ((Spar))|Chat with / spar against someone in your tile (picked for you; nobody dies in a spar). Greyed out when nobody suitable is here.|`talk` `spar`",
                "row ((People here))|Everyone in your tile, in the same list as the Browser (its buttons: [[browse]]); ((Open in Browser)) opens that list there. See [[locations]].|`people`",
                "h2 The command box and Help",
                "p The text box under the groups accepts **any command**, then Enter (Up and Down recall earlier ones). Examples:",
                "code list people 20\\npreview #12\\ngoto 1500 900\\nforage\\nwait 10",
                "p Commands that only look at things cost no time. ((Help)) next to the box opens this Help ({F1} or {H}); typing `help` in the box lists every command, and `guide combat` opens a page of this Help from the box.",
                "tip Walking into an unexplored tile reveals it and adds it to your [[locations|known places]]. Wild tiles get more dangerous with their level: look at the Level colour mode on the [[map]] before you go far.",
                "warn Beasts, crowds and strangers can attack you on the road. Before a long walk, check the route with ((Route…)).",
                "see map tile-info auto quests terminal-ref");
    }

    // ================================================================== Auto tab
    private static void autoTab() {
        HelpContent.add("auto", "Auto tab and the three modes", G5, "MANUAL, AUTO and HYBRID, and every button of the Auto tab.", "auto manual hybrid mode stop alerts do quest scripts watch time route duties all on all off autopilot npc",
                "lead The third tab hands the controls to the game, in whole or in part. Your character always runs in one of three **modes**:",
                "table Mode|Who decides|Use it when",
                "row **MANUAL**|You. The game waits for a command every turn. Your character still does its enabled [[duties]] by itself and tells you about alerts.|You want full control (the default).",
                "row **AUTO**|The game. Your character lives like an NPC: it works, travels, fights and follows quests by itself. Fights are decided by your [[duties#decision-policies|policies]].|You want to see what your character would do, or to skip a boring stretch.",
                "row **HYBRID**|Your plan. A [[scripts|script]] runs step by step, then control comes back.|You have a specific chore: “gather 500 herbs, go to the city, sell”.",
                "p The current mode is in the status bar. Attacks, breakthroughs and (if you chose so) new alerts **interrupt** AUTO and scripts and give control back, so nothing important passes unseen.",
                "img dock-auto.png|The Auto tab.",
                "h2 What is my character doing?",
                "p The card at the top says the current mode, what the character is busy with (the quest it is on, or where it is walking), where it is, which duties are on and how many alerts wait. ((◀ Take control back)) (`stop`) stops AUTO or a running script and gives the controls back; it is greyed out in MANUAL.",
                "h2 Mode",
                "table Button|What it does|Command",
                "row ((Manual))|You decide every turn (the default). If a script is running it stops the script first.|`manual`",
                "row ((Auto))|Switch to AUTO until you take control back. The number box (**Auto for N turns**) limits it: 52 turns is a year, 0 means until stopped.|`auto` (`auto 50` = for 50 turns)",
                "row ((Hybrid))|Run your script; with no script yet it opens the editor. See [[hybrid]].|`script run`",
                "p The lit button is the mode in force. A **single click** is all it takes to change mode, and ((◀ Take control back)) is always one click away.",
                "h2 Chores (duties)",
                "table Button|What it does|Command",
                "row ((Duties))|The Duties window: which chores your character does by itself. See [[duties]].|{U}, `duties`",
                "row ((Policies))|The same window on its Policies tab: how fights and prompts are answered when you are away.|`policy`",
                "row ((All on)) ((All off))|Switch **every** duty on / off.|`duties all on` `duties all off`",
                "h2 Scripts and quests",
                "table Button|What it does|Command",
                "row ((Scripts))|The script editor for HYBRID mode. See [[scripts]].|{T}, `script ...`",
                "row ((Run script)) ((Resume))|Run the script from the top / continue where it stopped.|`script run` `script resume`",
                "row ((Quest Log))|The quest window. See [[quests]].|{Q}",
                "row ((Alerts))|The quest window on the alerts that wait for you to do yourself.|`inbox`",
                "row ((Notices))|Everything the game told you, newest first.|{N}",
                "tip **Watch** (Move tab) accepts targets: `watch 200 for breakthrough,war` or `watch until year 120`. Categories: breakthrough, level, death, war, family, alert, danger, auction, fortune, reincarnation. An attack always stops it. See [[terminal-topics|the manual pages]].",
                "warn In AUTO your character may take fights you would refuse. Set your [[duties#decision-policies|decision policies]] first (spare or kill, flee, Forbidden Technique), and keep Settings, ~~Ask before risky actions~~ on.",
                "see quests duties scripts hybrid");

        HelpContent.add("hybrid", "Hybrid mode", G5, "A script plays your character, then hands control back.", "hybrid script mode interrupt resume return auto manual plan",
                "lead **HYBRID** is the middle way between clicking every turn and letting the game decide: you write a short plan, the character carries it out one turn at a time, and when it ends you are back in charge.",
                "h2 How it works",
                "# Open the editor with ((Scripts)) on the Auto tab (or {T}), add steps, press ((▶ Run)). The mode becomes HYBRID.",
                "# Each turn the character does one step of the plan. [[duties|Duties]] you switched on still run in the background.",
                "# When the script ends, or is interrupted, the mode returns to MANUAL (or AUTO if you set ~~after a script ends, return to AUTO~~ in [[duties#decision-policies|policies]]).",
                "h2 What interrupts a script",
                "* You were **attacked** (or challenged into a fight).",
                "* You **broke through** to a new major realm.",
                "* A step cannot go on: the tile has no herbs or metal left, there is no city to sell in, no beasts to hunt, a quest by that name is not queued.",
                "* Optionally, a **new quest alert** (the policy ~~a new quest alert interrupts a running script~~).",
                "* You pressed ((■ Stop)) or typed `script stop`.",
                "p After an interruption you can fix the problem and press ((Resume)) to carry on from the same step (`script resume`).",
                "tip The status line of the script editor tells you the mode and, after a stop, the last reason.",
                "see scripts auto duties");

        HelpContent.add("scripts", "The script editor", G5, "Every step you can put in a hybrid script, with examples.", "script editor steps goto wait forage mine craft meditate hunt sell quest repeat if end mode add delete clear run resume stop loop condition",
                "lead A script is a numbered list of steps. Press ((Scripts)) on the Auto tab (or {T}) to open the editor.",
                "img win-scripts.png|The script editor with a small script running.",
                "h2 The window",
                "table Control|What it does",
                "row The list|Your steps, numbered, with loops indented and their keywords in colour. While running, ◀ here marks the current step.",
                "row The step box + ((Add step))|Type a step, or pick one from ((Ready-made ▾)) (Move, Work, Trade and use, Loops and tests, Mode and quests) and change its numbers, then Add. ((Insert above)) puts it above the selected step.",
                "row ((Delete step))|Removes the highlighted step.",
                "row ((Clear all))|Deletes the whole script (it asks first).",
                "row Saved scripts (left)|Your saved scripts. Select one to preview its steps, then ((Load)); ((Save)) keeps the script on the right under the name it came from, ((Save as…)) under a new name, ((Rename…)) and ((Delete)) work on the selected saved script. The heading on the right says which saved script you are editing and shows ● when it has unsaved changes.",
                "row ((▶ Run))|Starts from step 1 (mode becomes HYBRID).",
                "row ((Resume))|Carries on from where an interruption stopped it.",
                "row ((■ Stop))|Stops now and returns control.",
                "p Editing is refused while a script is running. Each step is also available as a command: `script add <step>`, `script list`, `script del <n>`, `script edit <n> <step>`, `script insert <n> <step>`, `script move <n> <place>`, `script clear`, `script run|resume|stop`.",
                "p **Saved scripts**: `script save <name>` keeps the script in the `scripts` folder (one step a line, so you can also edit it by hand), `script load <name>` brings one back, `script saved` lists them, `script delete <name>` removes one. The window has the same buttons, and ((Edit selected)), ((Move up)) and ((Move down)) for steps.",
                "h2 The steps",
                "table Step|Does",
                "row `goto 15 15`|Walk to that tile (tile numbers 0-30 or displayed coordinates such as `1500 1500`).",
                "row `wait 5`|Stay put for 5 turns.",
                "row `forage 10` / `forage until 500`|Gather herbs 10 times, or until you carry 500 herbs. Stops if the tile has none left.",
                "row `mine 10` / `mine until 500`|The same for metal.",
                "row `profession`, `buygear`, `make food`, `learn 1`...|Any other word is a command of the game, run as it is typed (`cmd <text>` forces it). A walk or a wait inside a step finishes before the next step starts. The commands that would end or take over the script (`script`, `quit`, `save`, `auto`, `manual`, `watch`...) are refused.",
                "row `craft 5`|Use your profession 5 times (smith, brew pills, make arrays...) with the materials you carry. Without a trade `craft` opens the amateur menu (`craft weapon`, `craft clothing`, `craft pill`, `craft array`, `craft food|wine|poison`): amateurs use twice the material, make plain gear (best level 10 + skill, up to 60) and improve slowly.",
                "row `meditate 10` / `meditate until major 3`|Extra cultivation for 10 turns, or until you reach that major realm.",
                "row `hunt 5`|Hunt beasts in this tile up to 5 times; stops when none are left or they are too strong.",
                "row `sell`|Sell your sellable stock (you must be in a city).",
                "row `quest Go: home`|Walk to and perform a queued quest by its name.",
                "row `repeat 3` ... `end`|Do the steps in between 3 times. `repeat forever` never ends by itself (press Stop).",
                "row `if ss >= 1000` ... `end`|Do the steps in between only if the condition holds. Variables: `ss` (spirit stones), `major`, `level`, `age`, `herb`, `metal`, `items`, `potential`; operators `>=` and `<=`.",
                "row `mode manual` / `mode auto`|Leave the script and switch mode.",
                "p Every `repeat` and `if` needs its own `end`; the editor checks this when you run.",
                "h2 Example: a herb-selling round",
                "code goto 12 18\\nrepeat 5\\n  forage until 500\\n  goto 15 15\\n  sell\\n  goto 12 18\\nend",
                "p Walk to a herb tile, gather until 500 herbs, walk to the city, sell, walk back; five times.",
                "warn A script runs your character while you are not watching: stop at fights and breakthroughs is automatic, but risky routes are not avoided. Preview them with `route`.",
                "see hybrid auto duties");

        HelpContent.add("quests", "Quests and alerts", G5, "What quests are, which ones become alerts, and the quest panel.", "quest alert pin priority delete mute unmute waypoint do quest go there classify routine duty inbox queue qp qd qdo qnew",
                "lead Everybody in the world has quests: little goals such as “hunt a beast”, “sell your stock”, “battle your rival” or “join a sect”. Your character collects them too. The question is which ones **you** deal with.",
                "h2 Three kinds",
                "table Kind|What happens in MANUAL mode",
                "row **While travelling**|The box ((while travelling I:)) in the Quest Log (or `spotaction meditate|hunt|profession|surprise|talk|spar|forage|mine|explore`) says what your character does by itself in the spare moment of a turn when its routine would pick a plain spot quest: Meditate (the default), Hunt (beasts in the tile, else it meditates), Profession, Surprise me (a trade turn, a talk or a meditation: never a fight, never looting the dead), Talk (with the friendliest person here, never a fight), Spar (with someone who is no enemy: nobody dies), Forage, Mine or Explore (each falls back to meditating when the tile has nothing for it).",
                "row **Routine**|Silently ignored (Random, Profession, Hunt, Meditate, Forage, Mine, Interact, Find Person, Buy Gear, Go Sell, Return to Sect, Seek Territory, Hunt Cultivator, Visit Family, Courtship, Trade Run, Pilgrimage, Meditation Retreat, Tame Beast, Buy Pills, Attend Auction, Market Trip, Gathering Trip, Seek Teacher, Sightseeing, Courier Run, plain Hunt Monster...). AUTO does them.",
                "row **Duty**|Done **automatically** when its duty switch is on; otherwise announced like an alert. See [[duties]].",
                "row **Alert**|Announced with its target, coordinates, distance and the reason, and waits for you. You travel there and press **Do Quest**. Examples: Battle Rival, Revenge, Explore Dungeon, Heaven Treasure, Join Sect, Find Parent's Body, Reincarnate, and your own waypoints.",
                "p Alerts show up as the ⚑ badge in the status bar, as orange pins on the map (Quests layer), as a notice, and in the **Alerts** filter of the Quest Log.",
                "h2 How everybody else chooses",
                "p Every other cultivator, and your character on AUTO, picks quests by **character**, not by priority alone. Each quest belongs to a **family**: adventure, conflict, growth, trade, company, sect and city, meditation, journeys. Each person has a share of their time they mean to give to each family: a Merchant trades, a Friendly keeps company, a Musician and a Runner travel, a Spirit Vein meditates, a Leader keeps people round them; the young adventure and study, the old meditate and teach; the Evil fight more. A person who has had too much of one family **tires** of it (it is thought of less and waits in the queue unless it is urgent); one who has neglected a family is **drawn** to it.",
                "table Routine quest|What it is",
                "row Visit Family|Go to a parent, child, brother, sister or spouse who lives some way off and spend a few days: the bond grows, both cultivate.",
                "row Teach Disciple|Teach a disciple of your sect, a child or a younger friend: they cultivate and may learn your best technique; the teacher earns sect points.",
                "row Courtship|Spend a day with an unmarried adult of the other gender (a friend, or someone where you stand): each visit is a step toward marriage.",
                "row Trade Run|Take spare herbs and metal (Gatherers and Miners gather every turn) to a city whose treasury can pay; the city sells them on to craftsmen who run short.",
                "row Pilgrimage|Walk to a famous place (a story place, a spirit vein, Originis, a big city, a Celestial Trial) and meditate there six turns; now and then it brings an insight (a Potential Pill's worth of potential).",
                "row Meditation Retreat|Eight turns of meditation at a **spirit vein** (the old \"rest\"); a spouse or best friend is invited and you dual cultivate together. A character who stands on a vein often sits down by itself.",
                "row Tame Beast|Go to where a beast you could master lives and try to tame it (Beast Tamers keep several).",
                "row Buy Pills|Go to a city that sells the Cultivation Pill of your next realm and buy it.",
                "row Attend Auction|Travel to a city whose auction is coming with a lot you want and wait for it. Only people near the city (eight tiles) or on the way bid.",
                "row Found City / Found Sect|Go to the best site within seven tiles (a **spirit vein** first) and found it, if the world has room.",
                "row Market Trip|With no meal in the pack, go to a city market that sells one (and perhaps wine).",
                "row Gathering Trip|A Gatherer, Miner or craftsman goes to where herbs or ore are at least three times as rich and works there eight turns.",
                "row Seek Teacher|Go to the sect's master or a friend ten levels above and spend five days at their side: you cultivate and may learn their best technique.",
                "row Sightseeing|Go to a notable place five to thirty tiles away (a city, a sect's home, a dungeon, a spirit vein, a story place), look around for three days and learn the land near it.",
                "row Courier Run|A Runner carries word to a far city, which pays a small fee from its treasury.",
                "p A visit lasts four days, lessons four, a courtship three, a trade run two, a retreat eight.",
                "p You can run any of them yourself with ((Do Quest)) when you are there. A person who plays MANUAL is never sent anywhere by them.",
                "h2 The Quest Log (one window for everything)",
                "p There is **one** quest window. Open it with the ⚑ badge, {Q}, ((Quest Log)) on the Move, Me or Auto tab, ((Alerts)) on the Auto tab (the same window on the alerts), or the `q` command. It lists your active, target and spot quests; click a row to read it on the card at the right, double-click to go there (or do it, if you are already there).",
                "img win-quests.png|The Quest Log.",
                "table Control|What it does",
                "row **show:**|Filter: All, Alerts, Duties, Routine, Pinned.",
                "row ((Next alert)) ((▶ Go to top quest))|Select the next quest that waits for you / walk to the place of the first quest (same as the **Go** button of the status bar).",
                "row Columns|Kind (alert, routine, duty:city...), Quest, Target, Where (coordinates), Tiles (distance), priority (Pri), State.",
                "row ((Go there))|Walk to the quest's place, or chase the person it names (one tile a turn).",
                "row ((Do Quest))|Perform it now (you must be at its place; otherwise it tells you why not). Uses the turn.",
                "row ((Open target))|Open the person it names, or the details of the place.",
                "row **priority** + ((Pin priority))|Fix the selected quest's priority at that number, so it never fades away.",
                "row ((Always ▾))|Give every quest like the selected one the number in the **priority** box: all of this type, all about this kind of person or place (family, friends, enemies, sect mates, strangers, cities, dungeons...), or just this person or place. These are your **priority rules** (`qprio`); the Control window's Priorities tab lists and removes them. A pinned quest keeps its own number.",
                "row ((New quest…))|Make a quest by hand: any of the types, a person (id, name, or one you know) or a place (x y, the map tile, here), a priority, repeat. `qadd <type> [#id | at x y | here] [p=60] [repeat] [go]` does the same.",
                "row ((Unpin))|Let it age normally again.",
                "row ((Delete))|Remove the quest. It may come back unless you mute it. Deleting a pinned or your own quest asks first.",
                "row ((Mute this kind)) and the **muted** box + ((Unmute))|Remove every quest of this kind and never post it again; Unmute reverses it.",
                "row **new waypoint** label, x, y, pri + ((Add))|Create your own quest “Go: label” for a place (blank x/y = where you stand). AUTO and scripts walk there; in MANUAL it becomes an alert.",
                "h2 Typed",
                "code q                     numbered list of quests\\nqp 3 90                 pin quest 3 at priority 90\\nqu 3                    unpin\\nqd 3                    delete\\nqdo 3                   do it (you must be at the place)\\nmute Find Person        stop one kind\\nqnew home 1500 1500 70  a waypoint, priority 70\\ninbox                   the alerts",
                "tip A quest tagged ((Seek)) is one you created with `seek <id> talk|court|friend|fight|spar` (also the ((Go & talk)) and ((Go & court)) buttons in a character window, for someone in another tile): it follows that cultivator wherever they are. See [[browse]].",
                "see duties auto scripts map")

        ;

        HelpContent.add("duties", "Duties and policies", G5, "Chores your character does by itself, and how it answers prompts when you are away.", "duties policies decision kill flee forbidden duel timeout city sect shop craft upkeep bounty tournament social family loot toggle auto policy",
                "lead The ((Control)) window (Auto tab, button ((Control…)), or {U}) is the one place for what your character does for itself. Its first tab, **Chores and answers**, has **duties** (chores), **policies** (answers) and the **spot action**; the **Priorities** tab holds quest priorities and the breakthrough prompt.",
                "img win-duties.png|The Control window.",
                "h2 Managed duties",
                "p Each check box is a duty. **On** = your character does that work by itself, the way an NPC would. **Off** = it is yours: you do it, or you get an alert. ((All on)) and ((All off)) flip them all.",
                "table Duty|Covers",
                "row city|Buying a city array, enticing merchants, holding a tournament as lord, claiming or founding cities.",
                "row sect|Sect missions, donations, sect arrays, recruiting, diplomacy, raids, promotions, founding sects.",
                "row shop|Restock trips and selling stock for your shops.",
                "row craft|Gathering materials and working your profession.",
                "row upkeep|Using pills, buying gear, selling surplus, studying at libraries, Seek Potential.",
                "row bounty|City beast bounties and Bounty Hunt.",
                "row tournament|Registering for tournaments.",
                "row social|Grudge fights (Battle Dislike).",
                "row family|Having a child.",
                "row loot|Looting corpses.",
                "p Commands: `duties` (list), `duties all on|off`, `duties craft on`.",
                "h2 Decision policies",
                "p Used in **AUTO** and **HYBRID**. In MANUAL the game asks you instead, unless a timeout runs out.",
                "table Policy|Choices",
                "row after winning a fight|always spare / kill evil only / kill if wronged / always kill (default: always spare). `policy kill <0-3>`",
                "row try to flee when outmatched|on or off. `policy flee on¦off`",
                "row use the Forbidden Technique in a deadly fight|on or off (default off): it burns years of your life. `policy forbidden on¦off`",
                "row accept challenges|on = fight; off = try to slip away. `policy duel accept¦evade`",
                "row after a script ends, return to AUTO|off = MANUAL. `policy script auto¦manual`",
                "row who may join your squad|ask (a MANUAL player is asked each time; AUTO and scripts accept) / auto / never. `policy squad ask¦auto¦never`",
                "row an offer of friendship|ask / accept / decline. Declining costs nothing and is remembered; `avoid <n>` refuses one person (`unavoid` lifts it). `policy friends ask¦accept¦decline`",
                "row feeding your companion beasts|leftovers (default: food beyond 3 portions) / always (the best food whenever you have any) / never. One portion each four weeks; there is no hunger, a beast that is not fed simply grows at its own pace. `policy feed leftovers¦always¦never`",
                "row a new quest alert interrupts a running script|`policy alerts on¦off`",
                "row decision timeout|Seconds a prompt may wait before the policy answers for you; 0 = wait forever. `policy timeout <seconds¦off>`",
                "row list rows|How many rows a list shows by default. `limit <n>`",
                "tip A timeout is a good safety net if you sometimes leave the keyboard: the game never freezes on a forgotten window.",
                "see auto quests combat");
    }

    // ================================================================== World tab
    private static void worldTab() {
        HelpContent.add("world", "World tab", G6, "The fourth tab: every window that looks at the whole world, plus saving.", "world tab browse quests duties manage rankings market save saves settings digest chronicle notes tutorial export keys",
                "lead The last tab opens the bigger windows: lists, management, history, saving. All of them are **non-modal**: keep them open while you play.",
                "img dock-world.png|The World tab.",
                "table Button|Opens|Key|Page",
                "row ((Browse))|Select, filter and sort cultivators, beasts, shop stock, your bag and places; search everyone.|{B}|[[browse]]",
                "row ((Market))|This city's auction, goods market and library.|{K}|[[market]]",
                "row ((Manage))|Your shops, this city, your sect.|{M}|[[manage]]",
                "row ((Rankings))|Strongest, richest, most kills, oldest; charts; dashboard; rivalries.|{G}|[[rankings]]",
                "row ((Overview))|The whole world on one small map with cities, sects, dungeons and players.|{X}|[[world-overview]]",
                "row ((Painted map))|The world as an ink-and-wash painting with the named seas, ranges and rivers.||[[painted-map]]",
                "row ((Chronicle))|What happened, in one window: the world's record (searchable), Digest, your Journal and Recap.|{C}|[[chronicle]]",
                "row ((Digest))|The Digest tab of the Chronicle window: season, year or five years.||[[digest]]",
                "row ((Notes))|Your bookmarks and notes.|{Y}|[[notes]]",
                "row ((Time))|The date, the season, the next auction, your birthday: `time`.||[[turn]]",
                "row ((💾 Save))|Quick save, at once when the game waits for you or is paused.|{Ctrl+S}|[[saves]]",
                "row ((Saves))|Named saves: save, load, delete.||[[saves]]",
                "row ((Settings))|Text size, autosave, notifications, music, the story...||[[settings]]",
                "row ((Export ▾))|Save the map, a screenshot, the log or CSV tables to files.||[[export]]",
                "row ((Keys))|The keyboard shortcuts sheet.|{F1}|[[keys]]",
                "row ((Tutorial))|The welcome tour.||[[tour]]",
                "tip Quests, duties and scripts live on the Move and Auto tabs now; the Help button is on the Move tab (and the ((?)) in the status bar).",
                "see browse manage market settings saves");

        HelpContent.add("browse", "Browse", G6, "The list browser: cultivators, monsters, items, places, and search.", "browse list filter sort cultivators monsters shop items backpack places search anyone talk spar attack invite seek follow control preview copy id export csv buy sell go there waypoint",
                "lead Browse is the point-and-click version of the `list` command: one table per kind of thing, with filters, sorting and actions on the selected row. Open it with ((Browse)) or {B}.",
                "img win-browse.png|Browse showing the cultivators in the current tile.",
                "h2 The tabs",
                "table Tab|Lists",
                "row Cultivators|People in **your tile**, quest targets first.",
                "row Monsters|Beasts and awakened monsters in your tile.",
                "row Items|Three sub-tabs on item cards, with the same filters and sort: **Mine** (your backpack, with the change in your fighting level for gear), **For sale here** (the stock of this city's shops, with the price and the seller) and **Sell here** (your things priced by this city's shops against what the city pool pays). The Market window has the same shops as a tab.",
                "row Places|Places you know, with distance.",
                "row Search anyone|Anyone in the **whole world**, by name or `#id`.",
                "h2 Filters",
                "p The row of boxes at the top filters the list: name, level from/to (lvl ≥, ≤), alignment, type, price, affordable, distance, **extra** (any filter in the list language, e.g. `trait=Alchemist`), the sort column and ∨ desc, and **limit** (rows). Click a **column header** to sort by it; click again to reverse. ((Refresh)) reloads (the table also refreshes by itself).",
                "h2 Actions on the selected row",
                "table Tab|Buttons",
                "row Cultivators / Monsters / Search anyone|**One list of people in several places** (also People here, Relations and Family): the same filters, the same colours, a double-click or Enter opens the person's sheet, and the same buttons. ((Open)); ((Talk)), ((Spar)), ((Attack)), ((Invite to party)) act at once on someone **in your tile** (the status line tells you when they are elsewhere); ((Preview fight)); ((Chase)) walks after them; ((Follow)) / ((Unfollow)) is one button (the label follows the selected person); ((Control)); ((Seek ▾)) is a menu - Befriend, Talk, Court, Spar, Fight, **Kill (hunt down)** - that pins a quest to reach them anywhere (Kill: a chase with no give-up; when you stand with them you choose Kill, Spar, Spare or Not now, and a kill you choose spares nobody, children and monsters included); ((Go there)); ((Copy ID)); ((Export CSV)); ((Set as default limit)). Monsters: ((Open)), ((Attack)), ((Hunt here)), ((Preview fight)), ((Chase)), ((Follow)). In the tile lists the card and portrait of the selected person show on the right.",
                "row Items|Mine: ((Equip selected)), ((Equip best)), ((Sell selected)), ((Gift selected…)), ((Consume pills)). For sale here: ((Buy selected)) at the shown price, ((Buy gear)). Sell here: ((Sell selected)), ((Sell all (city))) and the toggle ((Only good prices)), which keeps only the things a shop here pays more for than the city pool.",
                "row Places|((Go there)) and ((Add waypoint quest)).",
                "row Search anyone|Type a name or `#id` and press Enter or ((Search)): anyone in the whole world. Only this list searches on demand; the others follow your tile.",
                "p ((Set as default limit)) stores the number of rows as your default for `list`.",
                "tip ((Preview fight)) (or `preview #id`) tells you the win chance, how lethal it is and your escape odds **before** you commit. See [[combat]].",
                "see combat character terminal-ref");

        HelpContent.add("manage", "Manage: shops, city and sect", G6, "Prices of your shops, the city you stand in and your sect.", "manage shops city sect found claim array tournament entice merchant promote recruit rules war peace alliance donate buy mission price rates restock",
                "lead One window, three tabs, for the things you own or lead. Open it with ((Manage)) or {M}.",
                "h2 My shops",
                "img win-manage-shops.png|My shops.",
                "p A **merchant** can run shops in cities. Select a shop, move the three sliders, press ((Apply rates)): **pay sellers** (what you pay people who sell to you, a fraction of an item's price), **charge buyers** (what you charge, a multiple of the price; always at least 0.05 above what you pay), **restock from city** (what you pay the city pool). NPC shops keep 0.55 / 1.50 / 0.30. ((Open / restock here)) runs a restock trip; ((Sell stock here)) sells what you carry. See [[economy]].",
                "h2 City here",
                "img win-manage-city.png|City here.",
                "p A sheet about the city you stand in: tiles for level, population, treasury, lord, shops (used of the slots) and libraries / arrays, a bar for the crowd against what the city can hold (OVERCROWDED in red), the tournament under way, and a table of the shops. Under it the buttons are grouped: **Rule the city** (((Found / claim city)) needs Major 2: it founds a new city on a wild tile, or claims a city with no lord; ((Buy city array))), **Events** (((Hold tournament)), ((Register))) and **Trade** (((Entice merchant))). See [[sect|Sects and cities]].",
                "h2 Sect",
                "img win-manage-sect.png|Sect.",
                "p A banner for your sect (or the one in this tile): master, members, level, cash, treasury, war and allies, your sect points and mission, then a table of members with their ranks in colour (gold for a Master or Founder); drag the bar between them to give either more room. The buttons are in four rows. **Membership**: ((Promote selected)), ((Recruit (random here))), ((Found sect)) (Major 2), ((Join)), ((Resign)). **Missions**: ((Missions)) (the board and your mission, `mission`), ((Take mission…)) and ((Take sect mission)). **Sect affairs**: ((Recruitment rules…)) (the master sets them), ((Donate to sect)), ((Buy from sect)), ((Buy sect array)). **Diplomacy**: ((Declare war here)), ((Peace)), ((Propose alliance here)). See [[sect]].",
                "see sect economy market");

        HelpContent.add("rankings", "Rankings and charts", G6, "Who is strongest, richest, deadliest, oldest; the halls of legends and of fame; charts; dashboard; rivalries.", "rankings charts strongest richest kills oldest population dashboard rivalries sects cities export csv png",
                "lead A window of league tables and charts (button ((Rankings)) or {G}).",
                "img win-rankings.png|Rankings.",
                "table Tab|Shows",
                "row Most famous|The hundred with the most **fame** (tournaments, hunts, kills of equals, crafting, breakthroughs, titles; it fades 4% a year). `ranking fame`.",
                "row Strongest, Richest, Most kills, Oldest|The top 100 living cultivators by that measure; the first three places are in gold, silver and bronze, and the **Title** column shows the title a place earns (see the next row). **Double-click** a row to open that cultivator. ★ marks you.",
                "row Titles (also the `ranking` command)|The boards that give titles, recounted every New Year: the hundred Strongest, the hundred Richest, the hundred with Most Kills. Rank 1-3 of a board earns its grandest title (Sovereign or Empress of the Nine Heavens, Lord or Lady of Ten Thousand Vaults, Crimson Calamity), 4-10 a lesser one (Pillar of Heaven, Master or Mistress of the Golden Vault, Nine-Hell Reaper), 11-100 the plainest (One of the Divine Hundred, Jade Vault Magnate, Butcher of the Hundred). You hold the best title you earn; your places are in your character window. Everyone on a board, and the thousand strongest, is spared by the epoch.",
                "row Hall of legends, Hall of fame|The legends of the chronicle (those who reached Ascension or beyond, as gold cards, then the firsts by year) and the runs that were retired into the hall of fame (the Retire button of Run stats writes one).",
                "row Charts|Population by year; cities, sects and shops by year; living cultivators by major realm. They fill in as game years pass.",
                "row Dashboard|The world at a glance as tiles and bars: population by kind, cultivators per realm, wealth spread (median, the top tenth, the top hundredth, the richest and how much the top 1% hold), deaths by cause (also `dash`).",
                "row Rivalries|Every sect (power, members, wars, allies, cash, joining rules) and every city (population, level, lord, treasury, shops).",
                "p At the bottom: ((Save charts as PNG)), ((Export rankings CSV)) and ((Export history CSV)) write files to the exports folder (see [[export]]).",
                "see chronicle world-overview sect");

        HelpContent.add("market", "Market: auction, goods and library", G6, "The auction house, the goods market and the technique library.", "market auction bid lot reserve goods food wine poison library learn technique sell technique buy",
                "lead Open it with ((Market)) or {K}. It looks at the city you stand in.",
                "h2 Auction",
                "img win-market-auction.png|Auction: lot cards with the reserve, whether you can afford it, and a bar towards the next auction.",
                "p Every city of at least 500 people has an **auction house**. The top of the tab shows the lots on the block (of the five that bring the next auction sooner), the weeks to it and your wealth; each lot is a card with its name, kind, grade, a gold HEAVEN-SENT mark if it is one, the **reserve** and whether you can afford it. Only luxuries are auctioned: **Heaven-Sent treasures** (see [[world-events]]), **Potential Pills** (they are never sold in shops) and the city's vintage food, wine and poison. Sell them in a city (`sell`) to put them on the block; you are paid 90% when they sell. You bid when the auction is held and you stand in the city: a maximum for each lot (0 passes), and the winner pays the runner-up's bid. See [[economy]].",
                "h2 Goods",
                "img win-market-goods.png|Goods.",
                "p Cities have a market of **food, wine and poison** portions (see [[life-arts]]). ((Buy 1)) and ((Buy 5)) buy the selected goods; ((Sell all my goods)) sells yours. Poison is only sold to Poisoners.",
                "h2 Library",
                "img win-market-library.png|Library.",
                "p A city library sells **techniques** (martial and movement) for spirit stones. The top table is the library, the lower one your own techniques (the equipped ones first). **Click a column header to sort** either table, and use the **filter bar** (name, type, potential range, highest price) on the library. Select one and press ((Learn selected)). A library keeps **one book for each type and potential**. ((Sell my best technique here)) sells your best unequipped technique of potential 4 or more for a **20% fee**: the book replaces the library's old one of the same type and potential, and you **lose** the technique. A learned technique starts at a fifth of its level and grows with use. See [[techniques]].",
                "see economy techniques life-arts");

        HelpContent.add("chronicle", "Chronicle and hall of legends", G6, "The world's history, searchable.", "chronicle history legends deaths wars founded cities sects breakthroughs search hall",
                "lead The Chronicle ({C}, or ((Chronicle))) records the world's big moments: deaths of strong cultivators, breakthroughs to Core Formation and beyond, new cities and sects, wars, calamities, legends.",
                "img win-chronicle.png|The Chronicle.",
                "* **Search** by text or `#id`, filter by kind (death, breakthrough, city, sect, war, world, legend...), or tick **only me**. Newest first. **Double-click** a row to open the first cultivator in it who is alive. A picture illustrates the selected event.",
                "* **Digest**, **Journal** and **Recap** are tabs of the same window (see [[digest]]). The **Hall of legends** is a tab of [[rankings|Rankings]]: it shows those who reached Ascension or beyond as gold cards (No. 1, 2, 3 ... with their level), then the firsts and legends of the chronicle by year, with a link to the person if they live. Living cultivators sometimes meditate on a legend and gain cultivation.",
                "p Typed: `history`, `history Wei`, `history #1234`, `history kind=war 50`; `journal` is your own story.",
                "see digest biography world-overview");

        HelpContent.add("world-overview", "World overview", G6, "The whole map in one picture with cities, sects and players.", "world overview map go to selected route preview details",
                "lead ((World)) or {X} shows the entire world as one picture: a gold square is a city, a purple diamond a sect, a red dot a dungeon, rings are player characters (yellow is you). North is up.",
                "img win-world.png|The world overview.",
                "p Click a tile to read what it is. ((Go to selected)) walks there, ((Route preview)) shows the trip, ((City / sect details)) opens the full report. The map shows what exists; the [[painted-map|painted map]] shows only what you have seen.",
                "see map painted-map");

        HelpContent.add("notes", "Notes and bookmarks", G6, "Your own notes on tiles and people.", "notes bookmarks note unnote go there",
                "lead ((Notes)) or {Y} keeps short notes about places and people so you can find them again.",
                "img win-notes.png|Notes.",
                "p ((Note this tile)) asks for a text for the tile you stand on. ((Go there)) walks to a place note or opens a person note. ((Delete)) removes one. Notes show as * on the text `map`. Typed: `note <text>`, `note #<id> <text>`, `notes`, `unnote <n>`. Notes are saved with the game.",
                "see locations map");

        HelpContent.add("digest", "Digest: what happened lately", G6, "A summary of recent weeks.", "digest summary recap lately season year",
                "lead ((Digest)) summarises what happened in the last season (13 weeks), year (52) or five years: tiles with how the population, cities, sects and shops changed (green ▲ more, red ▼ fewer), the chronicle entries counted by kind, the notable events by year with a coloured kind, and your own notices. The button of the span you are looking at is lit.",
                "img win-digest.png|The digest.",
                "p The box at the bottom, ~~Show when a saved game opens~~, makes a digest appear every time you load a game. Typed: `digest [weeks]`. The yearly **recap** pop-up is similar (see [[notifications]]); the **Recap** tab next to it shows the period so far and the last recap. The Digest, the Journal and the Recap are tabs of the Chronicle window ({C}).",
                "see chronicle notifications");

        HelpContent.add("saves", "Saving and loading", G6, "Quick save, named saves, autosaves, backups, crashes.", "save load saves autosave backup crash quicksave slot delete overwrite continue",
                "lead The game keeps your world safe in **save slots** in the `saves` folder. A save is written **at once** when the game is waiting for your command or is paused, and between two steps while it runs on its own (a save made while it waits for you repeats that week when loaded), by a background thread, **compressed** (an old world used to take over a gigabyte, now a fraction of that) and with a checksum. Saves from before the compression still load.",
                "table Way|How",
                "row Quick save|((💾 Save)) on the World tab, {Ctrl+S}, or `save` (slot `quick`).",
                "row Named save|((Saves)) window, ((Save as…)): letters, digits, - or _. Or `save <name>`.",
                "row Autosave|Every N game years (Settings; default 10), rotating `auto-1` (newest), `auto-2`... Keeps as many as Settings says (default 3).",
                "row On quit|((⏻ Quit)) and `quit` offer to save first.",
                "h2 The Saves window",
                "img win-saves.png|Saves.",
                "p A table lists each slot: name, kind, game year, population, players, when saved, size and state (**ok**, **ok + backup**, **DAMAGED**), with a thumbnail of the world when you select one. ((Save as…)) writes a named slot (asking before it overwrites one). ((Quick save)). ((Load selected)) loads (asks first: anything since your last save is lost). ((Delete selected)) deletes a slot for good (asks). ((Open folder)) shows the folder.",
                "p Every save keeps a **backup** of the previous good copy; loading checks the checksum and falls back to the backup. If the game ever crashes it writes `crash.log` and a `crash-<time>` slot you can load. A status line in the status bar shows the last save.",
                "tip Starting the program with saves present offers a **Saved games** list: ((Continue selected)) or ((New game)).",
                "see settings status-bar faq");

        HelpContent.add("settings", "Settings", G6, "Every option in the Settings window.", "settings options text size font autosave confirm pictures colour blind high contrast tooltips hints music volume notifications toasts sound auto pause recap challenges mature start",
                "lead ((Settings)) on the World tab. Changes apply at once and are saved in `settings.properties`. Every setting can also be typed: `settings <key> <value>` (and plain `settings` shows them).",
                "img win-settings.png|Settings.",
                "h2 Saving",
                "* **Autosave every N years** (0 = off) and **Autosaves kept**.",
                "* **Ask before risky or irreversible actions**: leaving a sect, declaring war, deleting quests, attacking with bad odds, overwriting or loading saves. (`settings confirm on|off`)",
                "h2 A new world and the epoch",
                "* **People at the start of a new world**: asked when a new game begins (default 100,000; 10,000 to 150,000 is recommended). A smaller world runs faster and needs less memory on a weaker computer; outside the range you are warned and the number is still used. The world keeps its size when saved. (`settings startpop <n>`)",
                "* **Most cultivators before an epoch**: automatic (twice the starting population) until you choose a number (`settings epochpop <n>`, `settings epochpop auto`). **Memory alive that brings an epoch (%)**: an old world is thinned before it fills the memory the game may use (once a year the game measures what is alive; `settings epochmem <percent>`, 0 = off). **Slowest typical turn** and the **cull** are the other two limits. See [[world-events]].",
                "h2 Display",
                "* **Text size** 70%-250%. Everything, including this Help, follows it.",
                "* **Place pictures**, **Paint a picture for places without hand-made art**, **New pictures every launch**, ((Repaint all pictures)).",
                "* **Colour-blind-safe map colours**; **High-contrast theme** (black and white; restart to apply); **Tooltips on buttons**.",
                "* **Swap my gear for me in manual mode**: off by default, so in MANUAL mode you wear what you choose (empty slots are still filled). In AUTO the game always picks the strongest.",
                "* **Challenges per turn from strangers**: how many strangers and dislikers may challenge you in one turn (default 2). Rivals, arch-enemies and war fights always count.",
                "* **New characters start with their first ten years of cultivation**: on, a new human begins at the level an 18-year-old of that potential would have (like everyone else). Story starts always begin at level 1.",
                "* **First-time hints**: one-time tips the first time something happens (see ((Reset hints)) below).",
                "* **Story: the Palace of the Oldest Order** and **Pause the game while a story scene is open**: switch the whole story off (nothing of it runs or shows), or let scenes of ??? play without stopping the game. See [[lore]].",
                "h2 Music",
                "* **Music Manager**: the options of the music (on / off, volume, Next song, Sound source, the playlist, Play now) live in their own window, opened by ((Open the Music Manager…)) here or ((Music)) in the Me tab. See [[music]].",
                "* **Scroll speed (mouse wheel)** (Display): how far the mouse wheel moves every list, table and text window, 1 to 10 (5 by default); it applies at once.",
                "* **Show each fight live (stick figures)**: the same switch as the check box in the fight window itself.",
                "h2 Notifications",
                "* **Pop-up toasts** and **Sound (system beep)**, and one check box per kind of notice (breakthroughs, level-ups, deaths, war, family, quest alerts, danger, auction, hints, recap, fortune, reincarnation).",
                "* **Auto-pause**: the game pauses when this happens to you: attacked or challenged, breakthrough, a relation dies, war or peace, birth or marriage, new alert, danger, auction soon, good fortune. This stops fast-forward from running past something important.",
                "* **Summary pop-up (recap)**: off, each season, each year, or every five years.",
                "h2 Help",
                "* ((Open tutorial)), ((Reset hints)) (shows the one-time hints again) and ((Open exports folder)).",
                "see notifications music saves keys");

        HelpContent.add("notifications", "Notifications, toasts and hints", G6, "Notices, the bell, pop-ups and one-time hints.", "notifications notices toast bell hints recap auto pause history categories mute",
                "lead The game tells you about things that concern you in four ways: the **event log** (see [[speed]]), **toasts** (small pop-ups in the corner), the **bell** ((🔔)) with its history, and the periodic **recap**.",
                "img win-notifications.png|The notification history.",
                "* ((🔔 n)) in the status bar counts unread notices. Click for the window: a table of when (week), kind and text. Filter by kind; **double-click** a notice to open the person it is about; ((Settings)) jumps to the options.",
                "* **Toasts** fade after a few seconds; click one to open the person or the history.",
                "* **Kinds**: breakthrough, level, death, war, family, alert, danger, auction, hint, recap, fortune, reincarnation. Level-ups start muted. Switch any kind on or off in [[settings]] (`settings notify <kind> on|off`).",
                "* **Hints** appear once, the first time something happens (first alert, first breakthrough, first crowded tile, first auction). Switch them off in Settings.",
                "* **Recap**: a periodic summary card (each year by default) with a check box to stop showing it and a Chronicle button; the Recap tab of the Chronicle window keeps the last one.",
                "p Typed: `notices [n]` for the latest notices, `recap` to preview one.",
                "tip Tick **Auto-pause** in Settings for the events you never want to miss, then use [[auto|Watch]] freely.",
                "see settings speed digest");

        HelpContent.add("export", "Exporting", G6, "Map images, screenshots, logs and CSV tables.", "export png csv screenshot map image log history rankings run folder",
                "lead ((Export ▾)) on the World tab opens a menu. Files are written to the `exports` folder (change it in `settings.properties`: `exportDir`); ((Open exports folder)) in Settings opens it.",
                "table Menu item|File",
                "row Map image (small, medium, large)|A PNG of the whole map as currently coloured, with the layers you have on.",
                "row Window screenshot|A PNG of the main window.",
                "row Event log (text)|The event log as a text file.",
                "row Yearly history (CSV)|One row per game year (population, cities, sects, shops).",
                "row Rankings (CSV)|The top lists.",
                "p More: ((Save charts as PNG)) in [[rankings]], ((Export CSV)) in [[browse|Browse]], ((Save as PNG)) in the [[painted-map]], and the commands `export history|rankings|people|monsters|shop|bag|places|world|run`.",
                "see rankings browse");

        HelpContent.add("music", "Music", G6, "The game's songs.", "music ambient sound volume soundfont instrument song piece engine mood theme",
                "lead The game has seventy songs, written note by note, with a drum kit and 24 instruments (the suona, yangqin, sheng, xun, xiao, marimba, timpani and upright bass joined the first fifteen). **Open country:** The Long Road, Lanterns on the Reed River, Wind Across the Barley, Cranes Over the Marsh, Beyond the Last Pass, Caravan Bells at Noon, Kites over the Plateau, Dust on the Silk Road, Riders of the Grey Steppe, Rice Terraces After Rain, Haul Away on the Tow Path, Seven Roads to Market Town, Where the Wind Sleeps, Lone Rider at the Border, Reel of the Harvest Fair. **Cities:** Market of a Thousand Lamps, Tea at Dusk, Lantern Festival Night, The Moneychangers' Waltz, Rain on the Tile Roofs, Night Market Drums, The Scholar's Garden, Rooftop Courier, The Abacus Song, The Lanternmaker's Lullaby, Guild Hall Fugue, Lion Dance in the Square, Last Round at the Crooked Crane. **Night and winter:** Snow on the Lantern Road, Moon on the Frozen River, Fireflies by the Stone Bridge, The Watchman's Lamp, Stars Over the Salt Flats, Lamplight on the Water, The Moon Does Not Hurry, Last Lantern Out, The Dream Weaver's Loom. **Danger:** Where the Beasts Wait, What the Lantern Does Not Reach, Eyes in the Cave, Thunder in the Ravine, The Sleeper Stirs, Bones Underfoot, The Hollow Court Drums, The Floor Is Alive, Three Footsteps and a Heartbeat. **War:** March of the Broken Banners, Ashes Before the Gate, The Red Tide Rises, Drums of the Eastern Gate, The Last Charge, Iron Rain, The Banner Bearers' March. **Sects:** Morning Bell, Mountain Hall; Incense at the Ninth Hour; Bamboo and Temple Rain; Disciples at Dawn; The Forbidden Scripture; Cloud Terrace Sutra; Sword Practice at Dawn; The Hall of Nine Gongs; The Elders Disagree. **Originis and the Palace:** A Tale Begins (the main theme), The Nine Lamps, Gates of Originis, The Voice in the Empty Seat, Noon at the Palace of a Thousand Windows, Banquet of the Nine Lamps, Procession of the Silent Court, Ambassadors in the Hall of Windows. A song follows the place you are in, fades into the next when you move somewhere that calls for it (at once in danger and war), rests for a while between songs, and is played a little differently each time (key, tempo, instruments, optional parts). `music list` shows them; `music play <name>` plays one. **Experimental:** `music gen` composes a new song in the same style and plays it (the game's own songs are written by hand and are better; this is for anyone who wants endless variety); if you like it, `music keep` adds it to your songs (they are kept in the `songs` folder), and `music drop <song>` removes one again.",
                "h2 The Music Manager",
                "p ((Music)) in the Me tab (or **Open the Music Manager** in [[settings]]) opens one window for every option of the music: music on / off, volume, ((Next song)), the **sound source**, newly composed songs, and the **playlist**. The list holds every song of the game, the songs you composed and kept, and the pieces of the musicians' concerts, each with a tick. **Nothing ticked by you means every song is played; untick the ones you do not like, or press ((Remove the shown songs)) and tick only the ones you love: from then on only the ticked songs are played.** A concert piece on the playlist is played as an ordinary song. ((Play now)) holds the selected song (it repeats after a rest) until you press ((Back to the playlist)). Typed: `music playlist` (lists them with numbers), `music playlist add|remove <n or name>`, `music playlist clear`.",
                "* Switch it on or off with ((♪ on/off)) in the status bar, or in the Music Manager; set the volume, ask for ((Next song)), and choose a **sound source**: automatic (a SoundFont if you put an `.sf2` file in the `soundfonts` folder), built-in instruments, or a specific SoundFont.",
                "* Typed: `music on|off`, `music next`, `music list`, `music play <song>` (only that song, `music play auto` to go back), `music gen [open|city|danger|night|war|sect|capital] [seed]`, `music keep`, `music drop <song>`, `music share <0-100>` (experimental: how often a song you have not kept yet may come up; 0 by default), `music volume <0-100>`, `music fonts`, `music font <name>`, `music engine auto|builtin|soundfont`, `music presets`, `music mix`, `music song`.",
                "h2 Concerts: listening to the musicians",
                "p A Musician (or anyone with an instrument) who plays in a city, at the seat of a sect or on the road is heard by the people around: the tile remembers the performance for two weeks (a street play) or four (a stage concert). ((Concerts)) in the Me tab (or ((Concerts)) next to ((Perform)) in the Backpack) opens a window with two tabs. **Playing nearby** lists every performance within 25 tiles with the musician, the piece, the instrument and its grade, and the distance; ((Listen / walk there)) walks to the place, and listens when you are in the tile. **The pieces** lists the whole repertoire by occasion, with a mark for the ones you have heard and a ((Replay)) button for them.",
                "* **Listening** takes the week. It plays the piece with the musician's own instrument (the better the grade, the richer the ornaments), warms your friendship with the musician (once per musician) and leaves you calm and inspired for four weeks. Typed: `concerts` (the list), `listen` (the performance in your tile) or `listen <n>` (the n-th of the list, when it is in your tile), `settings concerts on|off` (a notice when someone starts to play where you stand).",
                "* **The repertoire** has 43 pieces: twelve first pieces and thirty-one written for the occasions of the world (market, road, water, the four seasons, tavern, lament, festival, night, study, wedding, temple, war), six of them **masterpieces** (about two minutes, with a string and choir layer) played only by a master of grade 8 or more, or on a Heaven-Sent instrument, about one concert in five. The place decides the occasion: the seat of a sect plays temple music (war music in wartime), a city plays by the quarter of the year, a spirit vein asks for temple music, a haunted ground for a lament, a lair or a trial for war, the water for water music. `music concerts` lists all the pieces (+ heard, * masterpiece); `music concert <name>` replays one.",
                "note With no audio device the music silently stays off and the button says “♪ no sound”.",
                "see settings status-bar");
    }
}
